1472	Configuring aliases for URLs		11.3.11
1473	What is a URL?		11.3.11
1474	URL is the abbreviation for "Uniform Resource Locator", which is the page's address on the web. It is the "name" by which a browser identifies a page to display. In the example "Visit us at <em>example.com</em>.", <em>https://example.com</em> would be the URL for the home page of your website. Users use URLs to locate content on the web.		11.3.11
1475	What is a path?		11.3.11
1476	A path is the unique, last part of the URL for a specific function or piece of content. For example, for a page whose full URL is <em>https://example.com/node/7</em>, the path is <em>node/7</em>. Here are some examples of paths you might find in your site:		11.3.11
1477	node/7: Path to a particular content item.		11.3.11
1478	taxonomy/term/6: Path to a taxonomy term page.		11.3.11
1479	user/3: Path to a user profile page.		11.3.11
1480	What is an alias?		11.3.11
1481	The core software allows you to provide more understandable URLs for pages on your site, which are called <em>aliases</em>. For example, if you have an "About Us" page with the path <em>node/7</em>, you can set up an alias of <em>about</em> so that your visitors will see it as <em>https://www.example.com/about</em>.		11.3.11
1482	Overview of configuring paths, aliases, and URLs		11.3.11
1483	The core Path module provides the URL aliasing functionality. The contributed <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/pathauto">Pathauto</a> module allows you to configure automatically-generated URL aliases for content items and other pages. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.		11.3.11
1484	Additional resources		11.3.11
1485	Concept: Paths, Aliases, and URLs (Drupal User Guide)		11.3.11
1486	Using contextual links		11.3.11
1487	Goal		11.3.11
1488	Use contextual links to access administrative tasks without navigating the administrative menu.		11.3.11
1489	What are contextual links?		11.3.11
1490	<em>Contextual links</em> give users with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission quick access to administrative tasks related to areas of non-administrative pages. For example, if a page on your site displays a block, the block would have a contextual link that would allow users with permission to configure the block. If the block contains a menu or a view, it would also have a contextual link for editing the menu links or the view. Clicking a contextual link takes you to the related administrative page directly, without needing to navigate through the administrative menu system.		11.3.11
1491	Steps		11.3.11
1492	Make sure that the core Contextual Links module is installed, and that you have a role with the <em>Use contextual links</em> permission. Optionally, make sure that a toolbar module is installed (either the core Toolbar module or a contributed module replacement).		11.3.11
1493	Visit a non-administrative page on your site, such as the home page.		11.3.11
1494	Locate a block or another area on the page that you want to edit or configure.		11.3.11
1495	Make the contextual links button visible by hovering your mouse over that area in the page. In most themes, this button looks like a pencil and is placed in the upper right corner of the page area (upper left for right-to-left languages), and hovering will also temporarily outline the affected area. Alternatively, click the contextual links toggle button on the right end of the toolbar (left end for right-to-left languages), which will make all contextual link buttons on the page visible until it is clicked again.		11.3.11
1496	While the contextual links button for the area of interest is visible, click the button to display the list of links for that area. Click a link in the list to visit the corresponding administrative page.		11.3.11
1497	Complete your administrative task and save your settings, or cancel the action. You should be returned to the page you started from.		11.3.11
1498	Managing and deploying configuration		11.3.11
1499	What is the configuration system?		11.3.11
1500	The configuration system provides the ability for administrators to customize the site, and to move and synchronize configuration changes between development sites and the live site. It does this in 2 ways:		11.3.11
1501	Providing storage for configuration		11.3.11
1502	Providing a process in which configuration changes can be imported and exported between instances of the same site; for example, from "dev" to "staging" to "live"		11.3.11
1503	What is configuration data?		11.3.11
1504	Configuration data describes settings that define how your site behaves or is displayed. For example, when a site administrator updates settings using an administrative form, these settings are stored as configuration data. Configuration data describes settings as simple as a site name and as complex as a view or image style.		11.3.11
1505	What kinds of configuration are there?		11.3.11
1506	Active configuration		11.3.11
1507	Active configuration is the current working configuration of a site. Storage of active configuration is defined by the site, and resides in the database by default.		11.3.11
1508	Simple configuration		11.3.11
1509	A simple configuration item is a group of settings, such as the settings for a module or theme. Each simple configuration item has its own unique structure.		11.3.11
1510	Configuration entities		11.3.11
1511	Configuration entities are user-defined configuration items grouped by type, such as views, image styles, and content types. Each configuration entity within a type has a similar structure.		11.3.11
1512	Default configuration		11.3.11
1513	Default configuration can be defined by a module, theme, or installation profile in its <em>config/install</em> or <em>config/optional</em> directories. Configuration is provided in YAML files (file extension .yml); YAML is a human-readable data serialization standard that is used by the core software for several purposes. Once the default configuration has been imported into the site's active configuration (through installing the extension), that configuration is owned by the site, not the extension. This means that future updates of the extension will not override the site's active configuration for that extension.		11.3.11
1514	What is configuration synchronization?		11.3.11
1515	Configuration synchronization is the process of exporting and importing configuration to keep configuration synchronized between different versions of a site; for example, between a development site and the live site.		11.3.11
1516	Each site has unique identifier, also called a <em>UUID</em>, which identifies the site to the system in any instance of the site, as long as the site instances have been reproduced as clones (cloning is when the codebase and database are copied to create a new site instance). When site instances are cloned, a "dev" instance of the site has the same UUID as the "live" instance. When site instances share the same UUID, configuration can be exported from one instance to another.		11.3.11
1517	The following list contains terms and concepts related to configuration synchronization:		11.3.11
1518	Exported configuration		11.3.11
1519	When configuration is exported, the active configuration is exported as a set of files in YAML format. When using the <em>Configuration synchronization</em> administrative UI, configuration can be exported as a full-export or single-item archive. This archive can then be imported into the destination site instance.		11.3.11
1520	Imported configuration		11.3.11
1521	Imported configuration is configuration that has been exported from another instance of the site (the "source") and is now being imported into another site instance (the "destination"), thereby updating its active configuration to match the imported configuration data set.		11.3.11
1522	Configuration sync directory		11.3.11
1523	The configuration sync directory location is set in the site's <em>settings.php</em> file. When configuration is exported, the active configuration is exported and described in YAML files which are stored in the configuration sync directory. After the first export, the system compares the site's active configuration with the configuration data in the sync directory and will only export active configuration items that are different than their counterparts in the sync directory.		11.3.11
1524	Managing configuration overview		11.3.11
1525	Configuration management tasks, such as exporting or importing configuration and synchronizing configuration, can be done either through the administrative UI provided by the core Configuration Manager module or a command-line interface (CLI) tool. Defining a configuration sync directory path other than the default value requires read/write access to the site's <em>settings.php</em> file.		11.3.11
1526	Most modules and themes also provide settings forms for updating the configuration they provide. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.		11.3.11
1527	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/configuration-management/workflow-using-drush">Configuration Management: Workflow using Drush</a>		11.3.11
1528	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/understanding-data.html">Concept: Types of Data (Drupal User Guide)</a>		11.3.11
1529	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/install-dev-sites.html">Concept: Development Sites (Drupal User Guide)</a>		11.3.11
1530	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/install-dev-making.html">Making a Development Site (Drupal User Guide)</a>		11.3.11
1531	Creating a content item		11.3.11
1532	Access the Content overview page		11.3.11
1533	Managing content		11.3.11
1534	Create and publish a content item. See @content_overview_topic for more about content types and content items.		11.3.11
1535	Who can create content?		11.3.11
1536	Users with the <em>@content_permissions_link</em> permission can visit the <em>Content</em> page as described in this topic. Each content type has its own create permissions. For example, to create content of type Article, a user would need the Article: Create new content permission. In addition, users with the <em>Bypass content access control</em> or <em>Administer content</em> permission can create content items of all types. Some contributed modules change the permission structure for creating content.		11.3.11
1537	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>@content_link</em>.		11.3.11
1538	Click <em>Add content</em>.		11.3.11
1539	If there is more than one content type defined on your site that you have permission to create, click the name of the type of content you want to create.		11.3.11
1540	On the content edit form, enter the <em>Title</em> of your content, which will show as the page title when the content item is displayed on a page, and also as the label for the content item in administration screens.		11.3.11
1541	Fill in the other fields shown on the edit form for this specific content type.		11.3.11
1542	Leave the <em>Published</em> field checked to publish the content immediately, or uncheck it to make it unpublished. Unpublished content is generally not shown to non-administrative site users.		11.3.11
1543	Optionally, click <em>Preview</em> to preview the content.		11.3.11
1544	Click <em>Save</em>. You will see the content displayed on a page.		11.3.11
1545	Basic Page Management (Drupal User Guide)		11.3.11
1546	Creating a Content Item (Drupal User Guide)		11.3.11
1547	Placing a block		11.3.11
1548	Configuring a previously-placed block		11.3.11
1746	Who can moderate comments?		11.3.11
1549	Place a block into a theme's region.		11.3.11
1550	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; @layout_link.		11.3.11
1551	Click the name of the theme that you want to place the block in.		11.3.11
1552	Optionally, click <em>Demonstrate block regions</em> to see the regions of the theme.		11.3.11
1553	Find the region where you want the block, and click <em>Place block</em> in that region. A modal dialog will pop up.		11.3.11
1554	Find the block you want to place and click <em>Place block</em>. A <em>Configure block</em> modal dialog will pop up.		11.3.11
1555	Configure the block and click <em>Save block</em>; see @configure_topic for configuration details.		11.3.11
1556	Adding a link to a menu		11.3.11
1557	Add a link to a menu. Note that you can also add a link to a menu from the content edit page if menu settings have been configured for the content type.		11.3.11
1558	In the <em>Manage</em> administration menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; @structure_menu_link.		11.3.11
1559	Locate the desired menu and click <em>Add link</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.		11.3.11
1560	Enter the <em>Menu link title</em> to be displayed.		11.3.11
1561	Enter the <em>Link</em>, one of the following:		11.3.11
1562	An internal path, such as <em>/node/add</em>		11.3.11
1563	A full external URL		11.3.11
1564	Start typing the title of a content item and select it when the full title comes up		11.3.11
1565	<em>&lt;nolink&gt;</em> to display the <em>Menu link title</em> as plain text without a link		11.3.11
1566	<em>&lt;front&gt;</em> to link to the front page of your site		11.3.11
1567	Make sure that <em>Enabled</em> is checked; if not, the menu link will not be displayed.		11.3.11
1568	Optionally, enter a <em>Description</em>, which will be displayed when a user hovers over the link.		11.3.11
1569	Optionally, check <em>Show as expanded</em> to automatically show the children of this link (if any) when this link is shown.		11.3.11
1570	Optionally, select the <em>Parent link</em>, if this menu link should be a child of another menu link.		11.3.11
1571	Click <em>Save</em>. You will be returned to the <em>Add link</em> page to add another link.		11.3.11
1572	Locate the menu you just added a link to and click <em>Edit</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.		11.3.11
1573	Verify that the order of links is correct. If it is not, drag menu links until the order is correct, and click <em>Save</em>.		11.3.11
1574	Enabling and disabling maintenance mode		11.3.11
1575	Clearing the site cache		11.3.11
1576	Put your site in maintenance mode to perform maintenance operations, and then return to normal mode when finished.		11.3.11
1577	What is maintenance mode?		11.3.11
1578	When your site is in maintenance mode, most site visitors will see a simple maintenance mode message page, rather than being able to use the full functionality of the site. Users with <em>Use the site in maintenance mode</em> permission who are already logged in will be able to use the full site, and the log in page at <em>/user</em> will also be accessible to anyone.		11.3.11
1579	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>@maintenance_link</em>.		11.3.11
1580	Check <em>Put site into maintenance mode</em>, optionally change the <em>Message to display when in maintenance mode</em>, and click <em>Save configuration</em>. Your site will be in maintenance mode.		11.3.11
1581	Perform your maintenance operations.		11.3.11
1582	Uncheck <em>Put site into maintenance mode</em> and click <em>Save configuration</em>. Your site will be back in normal operation mode.		11.3.11
1583	Clear the site cache. See @cache_topic for instructions.		11.3.11
1584	Disabling comments		11.3.11
1585	Administer comments and comment settings		11.3.11
1586	Configuring comments		11.3.11
1587	Managing content structure		11.3.11
1588	Turn off commenting for a particular entity (see @content_structure_topic for more about content entities and fields). Note that if you want to turn off commenting for all entities of an entity type or subtype, you will need to edit the field settings for the comment field; see @comment_config_topic for more about configuring the comment field.		11.3.11
1589	Who can disable comments?		11.3.11
1590	You will need the <em>@comment_permissions_link</em> permission in order to disable commenting. You will also need permission to edit the entity that the comments are on.		11.3.11
1591	Find the entity you want to disable comments for, and edit it. For example, to turn off comments on a content item, you could find it by navigating in the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu to <em>Content</em>, filtering to find the content item, and clicking <em>Edit</em>.		11.3.11
1592	Under <em>Comment settings</em>, select the desired comment setting:		11.3.11
1593	<em>Open</em>: comments are allowed.		11.3.11
1594	<em>Closed</em>: past comments remain visible, but no new comments are allowed.		11.3.11
1595	<em>Hidden</em>: past comments are hidden, and no new comments are allowed.		11.3.11
1596	Save the entity.		11.3.11
1597	What is a content item?		11.3.11
1598	A <em>content item</em> is a type of content entity for page-level content, which can have fields that store text, HTML markup, images, attached files, and other data. See @content_structure_topic for more about content entities and fields.		11.3.11
1599	What is a content type?		11.3.11
1600	Content items are divided into <em>content types</em>, which are the entity sub-types for the content item entity type; each content type has its own fields and display settings. For example, you might set up content types for pages, articles, recipes, events, and blog entries on your web site.		11.3.11
1601	Overview of managing content		11.3.11
1602	The core Node module allows you to define content types, and add and edit content items. The core Field UI module allows you to attach fields to each content type and manage the edit form and display for each content type. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks. Many other core and contributed modules and installation profiles provide pre-defined content types, modify the permission structure for content items, and provide other functionality.		11.3.11
1603	Adding a bulk operations form to a view		11.3.11
1604	Administer views		11.3.11
1605	Add one or more existing actions as bulk operations to an existing table-style view. If you have the core Actions UI module installed, see the related topic "Configuring actions" for more information about actions.		11.3.11
1606	Who can edit views?		11.3.11
1607	The core Views UI module will need to be installed and you will need <em>@views_permissions</em> permission in order to edit a view.		11.3.11
1608	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; <em>@views</em>. A list of all views is shown.		11.3.11
1609	Find the view that you would like to edit, and click <em>Edit</em> from the dropdown button. Note that bulk operations work best in a view with a Page display, and a Table format.		11.3.11
1610	If there is not already an <em>Operations bulk form</em> in the <em>Fields</em> list for the view, click <em>Add</em> in the <em>Fields</em> section to add it. (The exact name of the bulk form field will vary, and may contain keywords like "bulk update", "form element" or "operations" -- not to be confused with <em>operations links</em>, which are applied to each item in a row.) If the bulk operations field already exists, click the field name to edit its settings.		11.3.11
1611	Check the action(s) you want to make available in the <em>Selected actions</em> list and click <em>Apply (all displays)</em>.		11.3.11
1612	Verify that the <em>Access</em> settings for the view are at least as restrictive as the permissions necessary to perform the bulk operations. People with permission to see the view, but who don't have permission to do the bulk operations, will experience problems.		11.3.11
1613	Click <em>Save</em>. The action(s) will be available as bulk operations in the view.		11.3.11
1614	Managing comments		11.3.11
1615	Managing user accounts and site visitors		11.3.11
1616	What is a comment?		11.3.11
1617	A comment is a piece of content, typically posted by a website visitor, which provides discussion or commentary on other content like blog posts and news articles. Comments are a type of content entity, and can have fields that store text, HTML markup, and other data. Comments are attached to other content entities via Comment fields. See @content_structure_topic for more about content entities and fields.		11.3.11
1618	What is a comment type?		11.3.11
1619	Comments are divided into <em>comment types</em>, which are the entity sub-types for the comment entity type. Each comment type has its own fields and its own form and display settings; each type can be used to comment on a single entity type. You can set up different comment types for different commenting purposes on your web site; for example, you might set up a comment type for recipes that has fields "How did it taste?" and "Did the instructions work?", and another comment type for blog entries that has only a generic comment body field.		11.3.11
1620	What is moderation?		11.3.11
1621	<em>Moderation</em> is a workflow where comments posted by some users on your site are verified before being published, to prevent spam and other bad behavior. The core software provides basic moderation functionality: you can configure permissions so that new comments posted by some user roles start as unpublished until a user with a different role reviews and publishes them. Contributed modules provide additional moderation and spam-reduction functionality, such as requiring untrusted users pass a CAPTCHA test before submitting comments and letting community members flag comments as possible spam. See @users_overview_topic for more about users, permissions, and roles.		11.3.11
1622	Overview of managing comments		11.3.11
1623	The core Comment module provides the following functionality:		11.3.11
1624	Posting comments		11.3.11
1625	Creating comment types; the core Field UI module allows you to attach fields to comment types and attach comment reference fields to other entities so that people can comment on them.		11.3.11
1626	Configuring commenting		11.3.11
1627	Moderating comments as discussed above		11.3.11
1628	See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.		11.3.11
1629	Creating a menu		11.3.11
1630	Create a new menu.		11.3.11
1631	In the <em>Manage</em> administration menu, navigate <em>Structure</em> &gt; @structure_menu_link.		11.3.11
1632	Click <em>Add menu</em>.		11.3.11
1633	Enter the title for the menu, which is used as the default block title if the menu is displayed as a block. If desired, also edit the machine name of the menu, which is by default derived from the title.		11.3.11
1634	Enter an administrative summary, which is displayed on the <em>Menus</em> page.		11.3.11
1635	If your site has more than one language, choose the language for the menu.		11.3.11
1636	Click <em>Save</em>. You will be on the menu editing page, ready to add links to the menu if the core Custom Menu Links module is installed; see related topics for further tasks.		11.3.11
1637	Adding a new display to an existing view		11.3.11
1638	Editing an existing view display		11.3.11
1639	Add a new display to an existing view. This will allow you to display similar data to an existing view, using similar settings, in a new block, page, feed, etc.		11.3.11
1640	If you are not already editing your view, in the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; <em>@views_link</em>. Find the view you want to edit, and click its <em>Edit</em> link.		11.3.11
1641	Under <em>Displays</em>, click <em>Add</em>.		11.3.11
1642	In the pop-up list, click the link for the type of display you want to add; the most common types are <em>Page</em> and <em>Block</em>. The new display will be added to your view, and you will be editing that display.		11.3.11
1643	Optionally, click the link next to <em>Display name</em> and enter a new name to be shown for this display in the administrative interface.		11.3.11
1644	Follow the steps in @view_edit_topic to edit the other settings for the display.		11.3.11
1645	Checking interface translation status		11.3.11
1646	Check the current status of interface translations, and see if there are any updates available.		11.3.11
1647	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @language_link.		11.3.11
1648	Look at the <em>Interface translation</em> column in the language table, to find the percentage of user interface text that has been translated for each language.		11.3.11
1649	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Reports</em> &gt; <em>@translation_updates_link</em>. This report is only available if the core Update Status module is installed.		11.3.11
1650	Optionally, click <em>Check manually</em> to update the report.		11.3.11
1651	View the report to find out if any languages have translation updates that you can download.		11.3.11
1652	Importing a single configuration item		11.3.11
1653	Single item		11.3.11
1654	Exporting a single configuration item		11.3.11
1655	Import a single configuration item in YAML format, such as one that was previously exported (see @export_single_topic). See @config_overview_topic for more information about configuration.		11.3.11
1656	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>Configuration synchronization</em> &gt; <em>Import</em> &gt; <em>@single_import_link</em>.		11.3.11
1657	Select the <em>Configuration type</em> that you want to import.		11.3.11
1658	On your computer or other device, copy the YAML-format configuration that you want to import to the clipboard.		11.3.11
1659	Paste the clipboard text into the box labeled <em>Paste your configuration here</em>.		11.3.11
1660	Click <em>Import</em>.		11.3.11
1661	Changing basic site settings		11.3.11
1662	Configure the basic settings of your site, including the site name, slogan, main email address, default time zone, default country, and the date formats to use.		11.3.11
1663	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>System</em> &gt; <em>@information_link</em>.		11.3.11
1664	Enter the site name, slogan, and main email address for your site.		11.3.11
1665	Click <em>Save configuration</em>. You should see a message indicating that the settings were saved.		11.3.11
1666	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>@regional_link</em>.		11.3.11
1667	Select the default country and default time zone for your site.		11.3.11
1668	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; <em>@datetime_link</em>.		11.3.11
1669	Look at the <em>Patterns</em> for the Default long, medium, and short date formats. If any of them does not match the date format you want to use on your site, click <em>Edit</em> in that row to edit the format.		11.3.11
1670	Adjust the <em>Format string</em> until the <em>Displayed</em> format matches what you want. (Date format strings are composed of PHP date format codes.)		11.3.11
1671	Click <em>Save format</em>. You should see a message indicating that the format was saved.		11.3.11
1672	Repeat the previous three steps for any other date formats that need to be changed.		11.3.11
1673	<a href="https://www.php.net/manual/datetime.format.php#refsect1-datetime.format-parameters">PHP date format codes reference</a>		11.3.11
1674	Configuring how user accounts are created and deleted		11.3.11
1675	Configure settings related to how user accounts are created and deleted.		11.3.11
1676	What are the settings related to user account creation and deletion?		11.3.11
1677	You can make it possible for new users to register themselves for accounts, with or without email verification or administrative approval. Or, you can make it so only administrators with <em>Administer users</em> permission can register new users.		11.3.11
1678	You can configure what happens to content that a user created, if their account is <em>canceled</em> (deleted).		11.3.11
1679	You can edit the email messages that are sent to users when their accounts are pending, approved, created, blocked, or canceled, or when they request a password reset.		11.3.11
1680	What are variables in email message text?		11.3.11
1681	<em>Variables</em> are short text strings, enclosed in square brackets [], that you can insert into configured email message text. When an individual message is generated, data from your site is substituted for the variables. Some commonly-used variables are:		11.3.11
1682	[site:name]: The name of your website.		11.3.11
1683	[site:url]: The URL of your website.		11.3.11
1684	[site:login-url]: The URL where users can log in to your site.		11.3.11
1685	[user:display-name]: The user's displayed name.		11.3.11
1686	[user:account-name]: The user's account name.		11.3.11
1687	[user:mail]: The user's email alias.		11.3.11
1688	[user:one-time-login-url]: An expiring URL that a user can use to log in once, if they need to reset their password.		11.3.11
1689	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>People</em> &gt; <em>@account_settings_link</em>.		11.3.11
1690	Select the method you want to use for creating user accounts, and check or uncheck the box that requires email verification, to match the settings you want for your site.		11.3.11
1691	Select the desired option for what happens to content that a user created if their account is canceled.		11.3.11
1692	Optionally, edit the text of email messages related to user accounts.		11.3.11
1693	Verify that the other settings are correct.		11.3.11
1694	Creating a content block		11.3.11
1695	Create a content block, which can later be placed on the site.		11.3.11
1696	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Content</em>.		11.3.11
1697	Open the @library_link tab.		11.3.11
1698	Click <em>Add content block</em>. If you have more than one block type defined on your site, click the name of the type you want to create.		11.3.11
1699	Enter a description of your block (to be shown to administrators) and the body text for your block.		11.3.11
1700	Click <em>Save</em>.		11.3.11
1701	Configuring error responses, including 403/404 pages		11.3.11
1702	Set up your site to respond appropriately to site errors, including 403 and 404 page responses.		11.3.11
1703	What are 403 and 404 responses?		11.3.11
1704	When a user visits a web page, the web server sends a response code in addition to the page content. A normal, non-error response has code 200. If the page does not exist on the site, the response code is 404. If the page exists, but the user is not authorized to visit the page, the response code is 403. The core software provides default responses for both 403 and 404 codes, but if you prefer, you can create your own pages for each.		11.3.11
1705	What other errors can occur?		11.3.11
1745	Decide which comments are shown on the website.		11.3.11
1706	Under some situations, your site can generate error messages. These can be due to user errors (such as entering invalid values in a form, or incorrect configuration), PHP runtime errors, or software bugs. Some errors may result in a <em>white screen of death</em> (a totally blank web page response); less drastic errors will generate error messages. You can configure what happens when an error message is generated.		11.3.11
1707	If desired, create pages to use for 403 and 404 responses. Note the URLs for these pages.		11.3.11
1708	In the <em>Error pages</em> section, enter the URL for your 403/404 pages, starting after the site home page URL. For example, if your site URL is <em>https://example.com</em> and your 404 page is <em>https://example.com/not-found</em>, you would enter <em>/not-found</em>.		11.3.11
1709	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>@log_settings_link</em>.		11.3.11
1710	For a production site, select <em>None</em> under <em>Error messages to display</em>. For a site that is in development, select one of the other options, so that you are more aware of the errors the site is generating.		11.3.11
1711	Running reports on your site		11.3.11
1712	Run reports to learn about the status and health of your site.		11.3.11
1713	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Reports</em> &gt; <em>@status_link</em> to see a report that summarizes the health and status of your site. If there are any warnings or errors, you will need to fix them. Take note of any upcoming highly critical security releases that may impact your site.		11.3.11
1714	If you have the core Database Logging module installed, in the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Reports</em> &gt; <em>Recent log messages</em> to see a report of the error and informational messages your site has generated. You can filter the report by <em>Severity</em> to see only the most critical messages, if desired.		11.3.11
1715	If you have the core Update Status module installed, in the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Reports</em> &gt; <em>Available updates</em> to see a report of the updates that are available for your site software. If <em>Last checked</em> is far in the past, click <em>Check manually</em> to update the report. Scan the report; if the core software or any modules or themes have security updates available, you should update them as soon as possible.		11.3.11
1716	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/security-chapter.html">Security and Maintenance (Drupal User Guide)</a>, which includes information on how to update your site's core software, modules, and themes.		11.3.11
1717	Editing a content item		11.3.11
1718	Find a content item and edit it, or update a group of content items in bulk. See @node_overview_topic for more about content types and content items.		11.3.11
1719	Who can find and edit content?		11.3.11
1720	Users with the <em>@content_permissions_link</em> permission can use the <em>Content</em> page to find content. Each content type has its own edit permissions. For example, to edit content of type Article, a user would need the <em>Article: Edit own content</em> permission to edit an article they created, or the <em>Article: Edit any content</em> permission to edit an article someone else created. In addition, users with the <em>Bypass content access control</em> or <em>Administer content</em> permission can edit content items of all types. Some contributed modules change the permission structure for editing content.		11.3.11
1721	Optionally, use filters to reduce the list of content items shown:		11.3.11
1722	<em>Title</em>: key word(s) from the title		11.3.11
1723	<em>Content type</em>		11.3.11
1724	<em>Published status</em>		11.3.11
1725	<em>Language</em>		11.3.11
1726	If you enter or select filter values, click <em>Filter</em> to apply the filters.		11.3.11
1727	Optionally, sort the list by clicking a column header. Click again to reverse the order.		11.3.11
1728	To edit the title or other field values for one content item, click <em>Edit</em> in the row of the content item. Update the values and click <em>Save</em>.		11.3.11
1729	A few types of edits can be done in bulk to multiple content items. For example, to publish several unpublished content items, check the boxes in the left column (right column in right-to-left languages) to select the desired content items. For <em>Action</em>, select the <em>Publish content</em> action. Click <em>Apply to selected items</em> to make the change. The other actions under <em>Action</em> work in a similar manner.		11.3.11
1730	Editing a Content Item (Drupal User Guide)		11.3.11
1731	Managing the search index		11.3.11
1732	Running and configuring cron		11.3.11
1733	Manage the search index, and make sure that the site is fully indexed for searching.		11.3.11
1734	What is the search index?		11.3.11
1735	The <em>Content</em> and <em>Help</em> search types provided by the core software pre-index their content and store the results in several database tables that are collectively called the <em>search index</em>. The process of indexing renders the content and breaks it up into words, which can then be matched more efficiently with keyword queries when users perform searches. Search indexing happens during cron runs; see @cron_topic for more information about cron.		11.3.11
1736	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Search and Metadata</em> &gt; <em>@search_settings_link</em>.		11.3.11
1737	Under <em>Indexing throttle</em>, select the <em>Number of items to index per cron run</em>. A smaller number will make cron faster and reduce the possibility of timeout; a larger number will make sure more of your site is indexed in fewer cron runs.		11.3.11
1738	Under <em>Default indexing settings</em>, enter the desired <em>Minimum word length to index</em>. Words smaller than this length will be dropped from both keywords when searching and content when indexing.		11.3.11
1739	If your site uses Chinese, Japanese, or Korean languages, optionally check <em>Simple CJK handling</em> under <em>Default indexing settings</em> to provide some support for these languages.		11.3.11
1740	Click <em>Save configuration</em>, and you should be returned to the <em>Search pages</em> page.		11.3.11
1741	Click <em>Re-index site</em> if you have changed the indexing configuration, or later on, if you believe that the search index has been corrupted.		11.3.11
1742	Follow the steps in @cron_topic to make sure a cron task has been configured.		11.3.11
1743	After waiting for cron to run several times, verify that the content has been fully indexed for searching. The overall status is listed under <em>Indexing progress</em>, and the status for each search page is shown in the <em>Indexing progress</em> column of the <em>Search pages</em> section.		11.3.11
1747	Users with the <em>@comment_permissions_link</em> permission (typically administrators) can moderate comments. You will also need the <em>Access the Content Overview page</em> permission from the Node module (if it is installed) to navigate to the comment management page.		11.3.11
1748	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Content</em> &gt; <em>@comment_published_link</em>. A list of all comments is shown.		11.3.11
1749	To unpublish comments, select one or more comments by checking the boxes on the left side (right side in right-to-left languages). Then select <em>Unpublish comment</em> from the <em>Action</em> select list and click <em>Apply to selected items</em>. If you select the <em>Delete comment</em> action, you can instead delete the unwanted  comments.		11.3.11
1750	To change the content of a comment click <em>Edit</em> from the dropdown button for a particular comment.		11.3.11
1751	To publish comments that are not yet visible on the website, navigate to the <em>@comment_unpublished_link</em> tab. Select one or more comments by checking the boxes on the left side (right side in right-to-left languages). Then select <em>Publish comment</em> from the <em>Action</em> select list and click <em>Apply to selected items</em>.		11.3.11
1752	Online documentation for moderating comments		11.3.11
1753	Adding a language		11.3.11
1754	Add a language to your site.		11.3.11
1755	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @languages_link.		11.3.11
1756	Click <em>Add language</em>.		11.3.11
1757	If your language is in the <em>Language name</em> list, select it and click <em>Add language</em>.		11.3.11
1758	If your language is not in the list, select <em>Custom language...</em> and enter the <em>Language code</em>, <em>Language name</em>, and <em>Direction</em> for the language. Click <em>Add custom language</em>.		11.3.11
1759	Wait for the translation system to be updated. Also, if you have the core Update Status module installed and translations are available for your language, wait for translations to be downloaded. You should be returned to the <em>Languages</em> page when this completes.		11.3.11
1760	Changing the appearance of your site		11.3.11
1761	What is a theme?		11.3.11
1762	A <em>theme</em> is a set of files that define the visual look and feel of your site. The core software and modules that run on your site determine which content (including HTML text and other data stored in the database, uploaded images, and any other asset files) is displayed on the pages of your site. The theme determines the HTML markup and CSS styling that wraps the content. Several basic themes are supplied with the core software; additional <em>contributed themes</em> can be downloaded separately from the <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/project_theme">Download &amp; Extend page on drupal.org</a>, or you can create your own theme.		11.3.11
1763	What is a base theme?		11.3.11
1764	A base theme is a theme that is not meant to be used directly on a site, but instead acts as a scaffolding for building other themes. The core Stable 9 theme is one example; other base themes can be downloaded from the <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/project_theme">Download &amp; Extend page on drupal.org</a>.		11.3.11
1765	What is a layout?		11.3.11
1766	A <em>layout</em> is a template that defines where blocks and other pieces of content should be displayed. The core Layout Discovery module allows modules and themes to register layouts, and the core Layout Builder module provides a visual interface for placing fields and blocks in layouts for entity sub-types and individual entity items (see @content_structure_topic for more on entities and fields).		11.3.11
1767	Changing site appearance overview		11.3.11
1768	The main way to change the overall appearance of your site is to switch the default theme. The core Layout Builder and Layout Discovery modules allow you to define layouts for your site's content, and the core Breakpoint module helps themes change appearance for different-sized devices. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.		11.3.11
1769	Extending and Customizing Your Site (Drupal User Guide)		11.3.11
1770	Theming Drupal		11.3.11
1771	Configure menu settings for a content type		11.3.11
1772	For an existing content type, configure the available menus that will be shown as options on content editing screens; links to content items of this type can be added to these menus during editing.		11.3.11
1773	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; @content_types_link.		11.3.11
1774	Locate the content type you want to configure, and click <em>Edit</em> in the <em>Operations</em> list.		11.3.11
1775	Under <em>Menu settings</em>, check the menus that you want to be available when editing a content item of this type.		11.3.11
1776	Optionally, select the <em>Default parent item</em>, to put links to content items under a default location in the menu structure.		11.3.11
1777	Managing media		11.3.11
1778	What are media items?		11.3.11
1779	Core media items include audio, images, documents, and videos. You can add other media types, such as social media posts, through the use of contributed modules. Media items may be files located in your site's file system, or remote items referenced by a URL. Media items are content entities, and they are divided into media types (which are entity sub-types); media types can have fields. See @content_structure_topic for more information on content entities and fields.		11.3.11
1780	What is the media library?		11.3.11
1781	The media library is a visual user interface for managing and reusing media items. Add media items to content using Media reference fields and the Media library field widget.		11.3.11
1782	What is an image style?		11.3.11
1783	An image style is a set of processing steps, known as <em>effects</em>, that can be applied to images. Examples of effects include scaling and cropping images to different sizes. Responsive image styles can associate image styles with your theme's size breakpoints. This allows serving images sized for the browser width.		11.3.11
1784	Overview of managing media		11.3.11
1785	The following modules provide media-related functionality:		11.3.11
1786	Media items and media types are managed by the core Media module.		11.3.11
1787	The core Media module provides a Media reference field to add media to content entities. The core File and Image modules also provide reference fields. It is recommended to use the Media reference field because it is more versatile.		11.3.11
1933	If you want the new user to receive an email message notifying them of the new account, check <em>Notify user of new account</em>.		11.3.11
1788	The core Media Library module provides the media library and the Media library field widget. With this module installed, the Media library field widget becomes the default widget for editing Media reference fields.		11.3.11
1789	The core Image module provides a user interface for defining image styles. The core Responsive Image module provides responsive image styles. Using the core Breakpoint module, and a breakpoint-enabled theme, these responsive styles can serve images sized for the browser.		11.3.11
1790	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/8/core/modules/media">Media module</a>		11.3.11
1791	Adding an image style		11.3.11
1792	Add a new image style, which can be used to process and display images. See @media_topic for an overview of image styles.		11.3.11
1793	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Media</em> &gt; @styles_link.		11.3.11
1794	Click <em>Add image style</em>.		11.3.11
1795	Enter a descriptive <em>Image style name</em>, and click <em>Create new style</em>.		11.3.11
1796	Under <em>Effect</em>, choose an effect to apply and click <em>Add</em>.		11.3.11
1797	Configure the effect on the next page. Most effects require some additional configuration after they are added.  For example, for the <em>Crop</em> effect, enter the <em>Width</em> and <em>Height</em> to crop the image to, and choose the <em>Anchor</em> point. Click <em>Add effect</em>.		11.3.11
1798	Repeat the previous two steps until all of the effects have been added.		11.3.11
1799	Drag to change the order of the effects. Then click <em>Save</em> to save the new order.		11.3.11
1800	The image style can now be used to format a field containing an image in your layouts or traditional field displays. It can also be used as part of a responsive image style. See related topics below for more information.		11.3.11
1801	Managing text filters and text formats		11.3.11
1802	Configure text formats on the site.		11.3.11
1803	What are text filters and text formats?		11.3.11
1804	A text filter is a processing step that can be applied to text, either to transform it in some way (such as converting URLs in the text into HTML links), or to defend against potentially-dangerous input from site users. A text format is an ordered sequence of text filters. Text filters are provided by modules; text formats are managed by the core Filter module.		11.3.11
1805	Text fields that have "formatted" in the field type name, such as <em>Text (formatted)</em>, use text formats. Users choose the text format when editing the field text; when the field text is shown on the site, it is processed by the chosen text format. Administrators can configure text formats and assign permissions for who can use each format. If the core Text Editor module is enabled, administrators can also associate visual editors with text formats.		11.3.11
1806	What text filters are available?		11.3.11
1807	Some of the more commonly used text filters are:		11.3.11
1808	Limit allowed HTML tags and correct faulty HTML		11.3.11
1809	Limits which HTML tags can be used; useful both for site security and enforcing site design.		11.3.11
1810	Convert line breaks into HTML		11.3.11
1811	Line breaks in HTML source are displayed as horizontal spaces. This filter converts line breaks into HTML paragraph and line break tags.		11.3.11
1812	Convert URLs into links		11.3.11
1813	Takes plain URLs in text and turns them into HTML links.		11.3.11
1814	Restrict images to this site		11.3.11
1815	For text formats that allow HTML image tags, restricts images to URLs within this site.		11.3.11
1816	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Content Authoring</em> &gt; <em>@overview_link</em>. If you do not have the core Text Editor module installed, the menu link and page title will instead be <em>Text formats</em>.		11.3.11
1817	Click <em>Configure</em> to configure an existing text format, or <em>+ Add text format</em> to create a new text format.		11.3.11
1818	Enter the desired <em>Name</em> for the text format.		11.3.11
1819	Check the <em>Roles</em> that can use this text format. Some HTML tags allow users to embed malicious links or scripts in text. To ensure security, anonymous and untrusted users should only have access to text formats that restrict them to either plain text or a safe set of HTML tags. <strong>Improper text format configuration is a security risk.</strong>		11.3.11
1820	If the core Text Editor module is installed, see the related topic to connect a text editor to this text format.		11.3.11
1821	Under <em>Enabled filters</em>, check the text filters that you want to use.		11.3.11
1822	Under <em>Filter processing order</em>, drag the filters to the correct order. Choose the order carefully; for example, if you have a filter that results in a particular HTML tag being added to the text, that should run after a filter that restricts HTML tags, to avoid deleting the new tags the first filter added.		11.3.11
1823	Under <em>Filter settings</em>, verify and adjust the settings for each active filter that has configuration options.		11.3.11
1824	Click <em>Save configuration</em>, which will return you to the <em>Text formats and editors</em> page.		11.3.11
1825	Repeat these steps if you have additional text formats to configure.		11.3.11
1826	Reordering and disabling menu links		11.3.11
1827	Disable menu links or change the order and hierarchy of menu links.		11.3.11
1828	Click <em>Edit menu</em> for the menu that you want to edit.		11.3.11
1829	Drag menu links into a new order, or check/uncheck <em>Enabled</em> to enable or disable menu links.		11.3.11
1830	Click <em>Save</em> to save your changes.		11.3.11
1831	Configure the settings of a block that was previously placed in a region of a theme.		11.3.11
1832	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Structure</em> &gt; <em>@layout_link</em>.		11.3.11
1833	Click the name of the theme that contains the block.		11.3.11
1834	If you only want to change the region where a block is located, or the ordering of blocks within a region, drag blocks to their desired positions and click <em>Save blocks</em>.		11.3.11
1835	If you want to change additional settings, find the region where the block you want to update is currently located, and click <em>Configure</em> in the line of the block description.		11.3.11
1836	Edit the block's settings. The available settings vary depending on the module that provides the block, but for all blocks you can change:		11.3.11
1837	<em>Block title</em>: The heading for the block on your site -- for some blocks, you will need to check the <em>Override title</em> checkbox in order to enter a title		11.3.11
1838	<em>Display title</em>: Check the box if you want the title displayed		11.3.11
1839	<em>Visibility</em>: Add conditions for when the block should be displayed		11.3.11
1840	<em>Region</em>: Change the theme region the block is displayed in		11.3.11
1841	Click <em>Save block</em>.		11.3.11
1842	Using the administrative interface		11.3.11
1843	Accessibility of the administrative interface		11.3.11
1844	Using in-line (quick) settings editing		11.3.11
1845	What administrative interface components are available?		11.3.11
1846	The following administrative interface components are provided by the core software and its modules (some contributed modules offer additional functionality):		11.3.11
1847	Accessibility features, to enable all users to perform administrative tasks. See @accessibility_topic for more information.		11.3.11
1848	A menu system, which you can navigate to find pages for administrative tasks. The core Toolbar module displays this menu on the top or left side of the page (right side in right-to-left languages). There are also contributed module replacements for the core Toolbar module, with additional features, such as the <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/admin_toolbar">Admin Toolbar module</a>.		11.3.11
1849	The core Shortcuts module enhances the toolbar with a configurable list of links to commonly-used tasks.		11.3.11
1850	If you install the core Contextual Links module, non-administrative pages will contain links leading to related administrative tasks.		11.3.11
1851	In-place or <em>quick</em> editing. In-place editing of configuration is provided by the core Settings Tray module. See @settings_tray_topic for more information.		11.3.11
1852	The core Help module displays help topics, and provides a Help block that can be placed on administrative pages to provide an overview of their functionality.		11.3.11
1853	What are the sections of the administrative menu?		11.3.11
1854	The administrative menu, which you can navigate by visiting <em>@admin_link</em> on your site or by using an administrative toolbar, has the following sections (some may not be available, depending on which modules are currently installed on your site, and your permissions):		11.3.11
1855	<strong>Content:</strong> Find, manage, and create new pages; manage comments and files.		11.3.11
1856	<strong>Structure:</strong> Place and edit blocks, set up content types and fields, configure menus, administer taxonomy, and configure some contributed modules.		11.3.11
1857	<strong>Appearance:</strong> Switch between themes, install themes, and update existing themes.		11.3.11
1858	<strong>Extend:</strong> Update, install, and uninstall modules.		11.3.11
1859	<strong>Configuration:</strong> Configure the settings for various site functionality, including some contributed modules.		11.3.11
1860	<strong>People:</strong> Manage user accounts and permissions.		11.3.11
1861	<strong>Reports:</strong> Display information about site security, necessary updates, and site activity.		11.3.11
1862	<strong>Help:</strong> Get help on using the administrative interface.		11.3.11
1863	Administrative interface overview		11.3.11
1864	Install the core modules mentioned above to use the corresponding aspect of the administrative interface. See the related topics listed below for more details on some aspects of the administrative interface.		11.3.11
1865	Translating user interface text		11.3.11
1866	Translate user interface text strings from English into a non-English language that is configured on your site.		11.3.11
1867	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Region and language</em> &gt; @translate_link.		11.3.11
1868	Using the filters, search for a string or set of strings that you want to translate; make sure to select the correct <em>Translation language</em> if you have more than one non-English language on your site.		11.3.11
1869	Enter new translations and click <em>Save translations</em>.		11.3.11
1870	Repeat these steps until all of the desired user interface text is translated for all languages on your site.		11.3.11
1871	Editing a URL alias		11.3.11
1872	Administer URL aliases		11.3.11
1873	Change an existing URL alias, to correct the path or the alias value. See @path_overview_topic for more about aliases.		11.3.11
1874	Who can manage URL aliases?		11.3.11
1875	Users with the <em>@path_permissions_link</em> permission can edit aliases.		11.3.11
1876	In the <em>Manage</em> administration menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Search and metadata</em> &gt; <em>@path_aliases_link</em>. A list of all the site's aliases will appear.		11.3.11
1877	Click <em>Edit</em> in the dropdown button for the alias that you would like to change.		11.3.11
1878	Make the required changes and click <em>Save</em>. You will be returned to the URL alias list page.		11.3.11
1879	Note that you can also add new aliases from this page, for any path on your site.		11.3.11
1880	Creating a URL alias for a content item		11.3.11
1881	Create and edit URL aliases		11.3.11
1882	Give a content item page a human- or SEO-friendly URL alias; you can follow similar steps to create an alias for a taxonomy term page. See @overview_topic for more about aliases.		11.3.11
1883	Who can create URL aliases?		11.3.11
1884	Users with the <em>@path_permissions_link</em> permission can create aliases. To follow the steps in this topic, you will also need permission to edit the content item.		11.3.11
1885	Locate and open the content edit form for the content item, or create a new content item (see related topics on creating and editing content).		11.3.11
1886	Under <em>URL alias</em> on the edit form, enter the desired alias (for example, "/about-us"). Make sure the alias starts with a "/" character.		11.3.11
1887	Verify that the page can be visited with the new alias, for example <em>https://example.com/about-us</em>.		11.3.11
1888	Modifying or deleting a user account		11.3.11
1889	Update or delete an existing user account.		11.3.11
1890	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>@people_link</em>.		11.3.11
1934	Optionally, change other settings on the form.		11.3.11
1935	Click <em>Create new account</em>.		11.3.11
1891	Enter all or part of the user name or email address of the user account you want to update or delete, and click <em>Filter</em>. A short list of user accounts, including the account of interest, should be shown in the table; if not, modify the filter text until you can find the account of interest.		11.3.11
1892	Click <em>Edit</em> in the <em>Operations</em> area of the account of interest.		11.3.11
1893	To delete the user account, scroll to the bottom and click <em>Cancel account</em>. Select what you want to happen to the user's content on the next screen, and click <em>Cancel account</em>.		11.3.11
1894	To update the user account, enter new values in the form and click <em>Save</em>.		11.3.11
1895	Export a single configuration item to a file. See @config_overview_topic for more information about configuration.		11.3.11
1896	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>Configuration</em> &gt; <em>Development</em> &gt; <em>Configuration synchronization</em> &gt; <em>Export</em> &gt; <em>@single_export_link</em>.		11.3.11
1897	Select the <em>Configuration type</em> that you want to export, and then select the specific <em>Configuration name</em> to export.		11.3.11
1898	Use your browser to copy the text in the box marked <em>Here is your configuration</em> to the clipboard.		11.3.11
1899	Paste the copied text into a plain-text editor on your computer or other device, and save it using the suggested file name below the text box.		11.3.11
1900	Installing a module		11.3.11
1901	Install a core module, or a contributed module that has already been downloaded.		11.3.11
1902	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>@extend_link</em>.		11.3.11
1903	Enter a word from the module name or description into the filter box, to make the list of modules smaller. Locate the module you want to install.		11.3.11
1904	Check the box next to the name of the module you want to install; you can also check more than one box to install multiple modules at the same time. If the checkbox is disabled for the module you are trying to install, expand the information to see why -- you may need to download an additional module that your module requires.		11.3.11
1905	Click <em>Install</em> at the bottom of the page. If you chose to install a module with dependencies that were not already installed, or if you chose an Experimental module, confirm your choice on the next page.		11.3.11
1906	Wait for the module (or modules) to be installed. You should be returned to the <em>Extend</em> page with a message saying the module or modules were installed.		11.3.11
1907	Managing content listings (views)		11.3.11
1908	Edit an existing view display, to modify what data is displayed or how it is displayed.		11.3.11
1909	Under <em>Displays</em>, click the display you want to edit.		11.3.11
1910	Find the section whose settings you want to change, such as <em>Format</em> or <em>Filter criteria</em>. (See @views_overview_topic for more information.)		11.3.11
1911	For sections containing lists (such as <em>Fields</em> and <em>Filter criteria</em>), to modify or delete an existing item, click the name of the item. To add a new item, click <em>Add</em> in the drop-down list. To change the order of items, click <em>Rearrange</em> in the drop-down list.		11.3.11
1912	For sections containing individual settings (such as <em>Title</em> and <em>Format</em>), there are often two links for each setting. The first link shows the current value; click that link to change the value. If there is a second link called <em>Settings</em>, click that link to change the settings details. For example, if your <em>Format</em> is currently shown as <em>Unformatted list</em>, click <em>Unformatted list</em> to switch to using a <em>Grid</em> or <em>Table</em> format. Click <em>Settings</em> next to your format type to change the settings for your chosen format.		11.3.11
1913	When you have finished changing all the settings, verify that the display is correct by clicking <em>Update preview</em>. Return to editing settings if necessary.		11.3.11
1914	When you have verified the display, click <em>Save</em>. Alternatively, if you have made mistakes and want to discard your changes, click <em>Cancel</em>.		11.3.11
1915	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/docs/user_guide/en/views-chapter.html">Creating Listings with Views (Drupal User Guide)</a>		11.3.11
1916	Uninstalling an unused theme		11.3.11
1917	Uninstall a theme that was previously installed, but is no longer being used on the site.		11.3.11
1918	In the <em>Manage</em> administrative menu, navigate to <em>@themes_link</em>.		11.3.11
1919	Locate the theme that you want to uninstall, in the <em>Installed themes</em> section.		11.3.11
1920	Click the <em>Uninstall</em> link to uninstall the theme. If there is not an <em>Uninstall</em> link, the theme cannot be uninstalled because it is either being used as the site default theme, being used as the <em>Administration theme</em>, or is the base theme for another installed theme.		11.3.11
1921	Installing a theme and setting default themes		11.3.11
1922	Install a core theme, or a contributed theme that has already been downloaded. Choose the default themes to use for the site and for administrative pages.		11.3.11
1923	Locate the themes that you want to use as the site default theme and for administrative pages.		11.3.11
1924	For each of these themes, if the theme is in the <em>Uninstalled themes</em> section, click the <em>Install</em> link to install the theme. Wait for the theme to be installed (translations might be downloaded). You should be returned to the <em>Appearance</em> page.		11.3.11
1925	Locate the theme that you want to be your default theme, which should now be in the <em>Installed themes</em> section. If it is not already labeled as the <em>default theme</em>, click the <em>Set as default</em> link.		11.3.11
1926	At the bottom of the page, select the <em>Administration theme</em> that you want to use on administrative pages. Click <em>Save configuration</em> if you selected a new theme.		11.3.11
1927	If you changed the default theme for your site, visit the site home page or another page on the non-administration part of your site and verify that the site is using the new theme. If you changed the administration theme, verify that the new theme is used on administrative pages.		11.3.11
1928	Creating a user account		11.3.11
1929	Create a new user account.		11.3.11
1930	Click <em>Add user</em>.		11.3.11
1931	Enter the <em>Email address</em>, <em>Username</em>, and <em>Password</em> (twice) for the new user.		11.3.11
1932	Verify that the <em>Roles</em> checked for the new user are correct.		11.3.11
2154	HTTP Basic Authentication module		11.3.11
1936	You will be left on the <em>Add user</em> page; repeat these steps if you have more user accounts to create.		11.3.11
1937	What is a user?		11.3.11
1938	A user is anyone accessing or viewing your site. <em>Anonymous</em> users are users who are not logged in, and <em>Authenticated</em> users are users who are logged in.		11.3.11
1939	What is a role?		11.3.11
1940	<em>Roles</em> are used to group and classify users; each user can be assigned one or more roles. There are also special roles for all anonymous and all authenticated users.		11.3.11
1941	What is a permission?		11.3.11
1942	Granting a <em>permission</em> to a role allows users who have been assigned that role to perform an action on the site, such as viewing content, editing or creating  a particular type of content, administering settings for a particular module, or using a particular function of the site (such as search).		11.3.11
1943	Overview of managing user accounts and visitors		11.3.11
1944	The core User module allows users to register, log in, and log out, and administrators to manage user roles and permissions. Depending on which modules you have installed on your site, the related topics below will help you with tasks related to managing user accounts and visitors.		11.3.11
1945	Creating a comment type		11.3.11
1946	Configure a content entity type/subtype to allow commenting, using a comment type that you have configured. See @content_structure_topic for more about content entities and fields, and @comment_type_topic to configure a comment type.		11.3.11
1947	Who can configure comments?		11.3.11
1948	In order to follow these steps, the Field UI module must be installed. You'll need the Comment module's <em>@comment_permissions_link</em> permission, in order to change comment settings for a comment field. You'll also need to have the appropriate permission for adding fields to the entity type or subtype that the comments are attached to. For example, to add a comment field to content items provided by the Node module, you would need the Node module's <em>Administer content types</em> permission.		11.3.11
1949	Follow the steps in the related <em>Adding a field to an entity sub-type</em> topic to add a field of type <em>Comments</em> to the desired entity type or sub-type.		11.3.11
1950	On the first field settings page, choose the <em>Comment type</em> to use for this entity type or sub-type. You'll also notice that the <em>Allowed number of values</em> field cannot be changed for comment fields.		11.3.11
1951	On the next field settings page, enter the desired settings for the comment field:		11.3.11
1952	<em>Threading</em>: whether or not the comments are collected by threads, with people able to reply to particular comments instead of to the content entity itself.		11.3.11
1953	<em>Comments per page</em>: the maximum number of comments displayed on one page (a pager will be added if you exceed this limit).		11.3.11
1954	<em>Anonymous commenting</em>: whether or not anonymous commenters are allowed or required to leave contact information with their comments (only applies if anonymous users have permission to post comments).		11.3.11
1955	<em>Show reply form on the same page as comments</em>: whether the comment reply form is displayed on the same page as the comments. If this is not selected, clicking <em>Reply</em> will open a new page with the reply form.		11.3.11
1956	<em>Preview comments</em>: whether previewing comments before submission is <em>Required</em>, <em>Optional</em>, or <em>Disabled</em>.		11.3.11
1957	<em>Default value</em>: each individual entity has its own comment settings, but here you can set defaults for the comment settings for this entity type or subtype. The comment settings values are:		11.3.11
1958	Online documentation for content comment settings		11.3.11
1959	Configuring site search		11.3.11
1960	What are search pages?		11.3.11
1961	The core Search module organizes site search into <em>pages</em>. Each page allows users to search a particular type of content with a particular configuration. The configuration includes specifying a URL that starts with <em>search</em>, a name for the page, and additional options for some search page types.		11.3.11
1962	When users visit the main search page (see @search_link), they will see the configured search pages that they have access to. Each search page has a search form on it, and the page will display search results after the user enters keywords into the form and clicks the search button.		11.3.11
1963	What modules provide site search?		11.3.11
1964	The core Search module provides the ability to configure search pages; search page types are provided by both core and contributed modules. The core modules that provide search page types are:		11.3.11
1965	The Node module, for searching content pages		11.3.11
1966	The User module, for searching user profiles		11.3.11
1967	The Help module, for searching help topics		11.3.11
1968	As an alternative to the core Search module's system of search pages, you can use contributed modules to provide site search. For example, the <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/apachesolr">Apache Solr</a> and <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/sphinx">Sphinx</a> contributed modules use third-party technology to provide site search.		11.3.11
1969	What are the limitations of the core Search module?		11.3.11
1970	There are two main limitations of the core Search module. First, it is not appropriate for very large sites -- if you have a large site, look into other search technologies like Apache Solr. Second, the Node search page type only supports exact keyword matching, which is not the behavior that most users will expect. You can improve this by installing a language-specific stemming module for your language (such as <a href="https://www.drupal.org/project/porterstemmer">Porter Stemmer</a> for American English), which makes it so that, for example, a search for the word walk would match pages containing the words walk, walking, and walked.		11.3.11
1971	What are the search permissions?		11.3.11
1972	Users with <em>Use search</em> permission can use the <em>Search form</em> block and <em>Search</em> page; this permission is required for any search configured in the core Search module.		11.3.11
1973	In addition to <em>Use search</em>, <em>View user information</em> permission is needed for searching users.		11.3.11
1974	In addition to <em>Use search</em>, <em>View published content</em> permission is needed for searching content.		11.3.11
1975	Users with <em>Use advanced search</em> permission can use more complex search filtering when performing content searches.		11.3.11
1976	Configuring site search overview		11.3.11
2153	Provides a framework for adding specific serialization formats for other modules to use.		11.3.11
1977	In order to configure site search using the core Search module, you will need to configure one or more search pages. You will also need to verify or alter permissions so that the desired user roles can search the site. (See @user_overview_topic for more information about roles and permissions.) For content search, you will also need to make sure that the search index is configured and that the site is fully indexed. Finally, you may wish to place the <em>Search form</em> block on pages of your site, or add the search page to a navigation menu, to give users easy access to search. See the related topics listed below for specific tasks.		11.3.11
1978	<a href="https://www.drupal.org/documentation/modules/search">Online documentation for the Search module</a>		11.3.11
1979	Managing height, width, and resolution breakpoints		11.3.11
1980	What are breakpoints?		11.3.11
1981	Breakpoints are the point at which your site's content will respond to provide the user with the best possible layout to consume the information. A breakpoint separates the height or width of viewports (screens, printers, and other media output types) into steps. For instance, a width breakpoint of 40em creates two steps: one for widths up to 40em and one for widths above 40em. Breakpoints can be used to define when layouts should shift from one form to another, when images should be resized, and other changes that need to respond to changes in viewport height or width.		11.3.11
1982	What are media queries?		11.3.11
1983	Media  queries are a formal way to encode breakpoints. For instance, a width breakpoint at 40em would be written as the media query "(min-width: 40em)". Breakpoints are really just media queries with some additional meta-data, such as a name and multiplier information.		11.3.11
1984	What are resolution multipliers?		11.3.11
1985	Resolution multipliers are a measure of the viewport's device resolution, defined to be the ratio between the physical pixel size of the active device and the <a href="http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Device_independent_pixel">device-independent pixel</a> size. The Breakpoint module defines multipliers of 1, 1.5, and 2; when defining breakpoints, modules and themes can define which multipliers apply to each breakpoint.		11.3.11
1986	What is a breakpoint group?		11.3.11
1987	Breakpoints can be organized into groups. Modules and themes should use groups to separate out breakpoints that are meant to be used for different purposes, such as breakpoints for layouts or breakpoints for image sizing.		11.3.11
1988	Managing breakpoints and breakpoint groups overview		11.3.11
1989	The <em>Breakpoint</em> module allows you to define breakpoints and breakpoint groups in YAML files. Modules and themes can use the API provided by the <em>Breakpoint</em> module to define breakpoints and breakpoint groups, and to assign resolution multipliers to breakpoints.		11.3.11
1990	Working with breakpoints in Drupal		11.3.11
1991	W3C standards for media queries		11.3.11
